"""Everyday symptom notes: joints, muscles, head, breathing, and ENT (batch 5)."""

# id, title, source, url, condition, triage, paragraph1, paragraph2, paragraph3
_RAW = [
    ("knee_pain_walking", "Knee Pain When Walking", "NHS - Knee pain", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/knee-pain/", "Knee Pain", "self_care",
     "People often say my knee hurts when I walk or why does my knee hurt after a stroll. Common possibilities include early osteoarthritis, a simple strain, or irritation of the kneecap after extra steps on hard ground. Pain is usually in the front or inner knee and eases with rest.",
     "Helpful first steps are shorter walks, supportive shoes, ice after activity, and gentle straightening exercises. Over-the-counter pain relief such as paracetamol can ease discomfort if it is safe for you. This does not name one disease.",
     "See a doctor if walking pain lasts more than a few weeks, the knee swells, locks, or gives way. Seek urgent care if you cannot take any weight after an injury, or the knee looks deformed."),
    ("knee_pain_stairs", "Knee Pain When Climbing Stairs", "NHS - Knee pain", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/knee-pain/", "Knee Pain", "self_care",
     "A common complaint is my knee hurts when I climb stairs or go downstairs. Pain under or around the kneecap often relates to how the kneecap tracks, weak thigh muscles, or early cartilage wear. Going down stairs is often worse than going up.",
     "Try a handrail, shorter steps, strengthening the front of the thigh, and avoiding deep squats for a while. Ice after stairs and activity that does not force a full bend can help.",
     "See a doctor if stair pain is getting worse, the knee swells after every flight, or you are over 50 with night pain. Seek urgent care after a fall if you cannot straighten the knee."),
    ("knee_pain_after_sitting", "Knee Stiffness After Sitting", "NHS - Knee pain", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/knee-pain/", "Knee Pain", "self_care",
     "Some people notice a stiff, sore knee after sitting a long time, such as at a desk or in a car. This can happen with early osteoarthritis, tight thigh muscles, or simple inactivity. The first few steps often feel awkward, then loosen.",
     "Stand and stretch every 30 to 45 minutes, gently straighten and bend the knee, and keep walking short distances through the day. Heat can ease morning stiffness.",
     "See a doctor if stiffness lasts more than 30 minutes every morning, several joints swell, or you also have unexplained fever or weight loss."),
    ("knee_swelling_after_activity", "Swollen Knee After Activity", "NHS - Knee pain", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/knee-pain/", "Knee Swelling", "consult_doctor",
     "A knee that puffs up after sport or extra walking may have irritated lining, a small cartilage injury, or fluid after overload. Rapid swelling within a few hours of a twist is more concerning than mild puffiness the next day.",
     "Rest, ice, a light compression bandage, and elevation reduce fluid. Avoid running until swelling settles. Do not drain a knee at home.",
     "See a doctor for swelling that returns after every activity. Seek urgent care for sudden swelling after injury, a hot red knee with fever, or inability to walk."),
    ("shoulder_pain_lifting_arm", "Shoulder Pain When Lifting the Arm", "NHS - Shoulder pain", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/shoulder-pain/", "Shoulder Pain", "self_care",
     "Many people say my shoulder hurts when I lift my arm or reach a high shelf. Common possibilities are rotator cuff irritation, a pinched tendon under the shoulder bone, or a stiff capsule. Pain is often on the outer upper arm and worse at night when lying on that side.",
     "Avoid repeated overhead lifting for a few days, use ice after activity, and try gentle pendulum swings. Sleeping with a pillow under the arm can reduce night pain. This is not a diagnosis.",
     "See a doctor or physiotherapist if you cannot lift the arm after two weeks, or pain followed a fall. Seek urgent care if the arm is numb, cold, or the shoulder looks square and out of place."),
    ("shoulder_pain_at_night", "Shoulder Pain at Night", "NHS - Shoulder pain", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/shoulder-pain/", "Shoulder Pain", "self_care",
     "Night shoulder pain that wakes you when you roll onto that side is common with rotator cuff problems and frozen shoulder. People also describe a deep ache after a day of reaching or carrying.",
     "Try sleeping on the other side with a pillow hugged to the sore arm, ease overhead work, and keep the shoulder gently moving so it does not stiffen.",
     "See a doctor if night pain lasts more than a few weeks or you cannot reach behind your back. Seek urgent care for chest pain with the shoulder ache, breathlessness, or sweating."),
    ("neck_pain_after_desk_work", "Neck Pain After Desk Work", "NHS - Neck pain and stiff neck", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/neck-pain-and-stiff-neck/", "Neck Pain", "self_care",
     "A stiff, aching neck after looking at a screen is very common. Holding the head forward for hours strains the muscles at the back of the neck and can send pain into the shoulders or a dull headache.",
     "Raise the screen to eye level, stand every half hour, and gently turn the head side to side. Heat and simple pain relief can ease a tight neck. Avoid forcing a crack.",
     "See a doctor if neck pain lasts more than a few weeks or travels down an arm with tingling. Seek emergency care for neck pain after a crash, or with fever, a severe headache, or confusion."),
    ("neck_related_headache", "Neck-Related Headache", "NHS - Headaches", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/headaches/", "Neck-Related Headache", "self_care",
     "Some headaches start at the base of the skull and wrap to the temples after a stiff neck or long time at a computer. This pattern is often called a cervicogenic or tension-type headache linked to neck muscles, not a brain problem.",
     "Gentle neck stretches, better screen height, and breaks from looking down at a phone often help. Heat on the upper shoulders can loosen the area that feeds the headache.",
     "See a doctor for headaches that are new, worsening, or wake you from sleep. Seek emergency care for the worst headache of your life, a weak face or arm, or a sudden stiff neck with fever."),
    ("lower_back_pain_after_sitting", "Lower Back Pain After Sitting", "NHS - Back pain", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/back-pain/", "Lower Back Pain", "self_care",
     "People often say my lower back hurts after sitting or I have pain on one side of the lower back after a long desk day. Prolonged sitting, a soft sofa, or a wallet in a back pocket can strain the lumbar muscles and joints. Pain is usually aching and eases when you walk.",
     "Stand and walk for a few minutes each hour, sit with both feet on the floor, and try a small cushion in the lower back. Gentle knee-to-chest stretches and staying active are better than long bed rest.",
     "See a doctor if back pain lasts more than a few weeks or keeps you from daily tasks. Seek emergency care for back pain with numbness between the legs, new bladder or bowel trouble, or weak legs."),
    ("upper_back_pain_between_shoulders", "Upper Back Pain Between the Shoulders", "NHS - Back pain", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/back-pain/", "Upper Back Pain", "self_care",
     "A burning or tight band between the shoulder blades often follows rounded posture, carrying a bag on one side, or long laptop use. Muscle fatigue in the mid-back is a common explanation, not automatically a disc problem.",
     "Open the chest by sitting taller, squeeze the shoulder blades gently, and take short walking breaks. Heat and simple pain relief can settle a tired mid-back.",
     "See a doctor if the pain is constant, follows a fall, or you also have unexplained weight loss. Seek emergency care for upper back pain with chest tightness, breathlessness, or pain spreading to the jaw."),
    ("hip_pain_walking", "Hip Pain When Walking", "NHS - Hip pain in adults", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/hip-pain/", "Hip Pain", "consult_doctor",
     "Hip pain when walking may be felt in the groin, the side of the hip, or the buttock. Common possibilities include early arthritis in the joint, an irritated tendon on the outer hip, or referred pain from the lower back. Groin pain that limits putting on socks is more joint-related.",
     "Shorter steps, a walking stick on the opposite side if needed, and avoiding deep sitting can reduce load. Gentle movement is better than complete rest.",
     "See a doctor if hip pain lasts more than a few weeks or wakes you at night. Seek urgent care after a fall if you cannot take weight, especially if you are older."),
    ("hip_pain_after_sitting", "Hip Stiffness After Sitting", "NHS - Hip pain in adults", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/hip-pain/", "Hip Pain", "self_care",
     "A stiff hip after sitting that eases after a few steps is common with early wear in the joint or tight hip flexors from desk work. Some people also feel it in the groin when getting out of a car.",
     "Stand regularly, stretch the front of the hip, and keep walking. A slightly higher chair can make standing up easier.",
     "See a doctor if you limp most days or pain spreads down the thigh. Seek urgent care for a sudden inability to move the hip after injury."),
    ("elbow_pain_gripping", "Elbow Pain When Gripping", "NHS - Tennis elbow", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/tennis-elbow/", "Elbow Pain", "self_care",
     "Pain on the outer or inner elbow when gripping a cup, typing, or turning a door handle is often tendon overload, sometimes called tennis or golfer elbow even if you do not play sport.",
     "Ease the gripping task, use a wider handle if possible, ice after activity, and keep the elbow moving gently. A forearm strap can reduce strain for some people.",
     "See a doctor or physiotherapist if gripping pain lasts more than six to eight weeks. Seek urgent care if the elbow is hot, swollen, and you have a fever."),
    ("wrist_pain_computer", "Wrist Pain After Using a Computer", "NHS - Wrist pain", "https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/hand-pain/wrist-pain/", "Wrist Pain", "self_care",
     "Aching wrists after typing or using a mouse are common. People say my wrist hurts after using a computer. Strain of the tendons, a bent wrist on a laptop, or early carpal tunnel tingling can all play a part.",
     "Keep the wrist straighter with the keyboard at elbow height, take typing breaks, and stretch the fingers backward gently. Night-time tingling into the thumb and first fingers needs a medical check.",
     "See a doctor if numbness or weakness develops, or pain lasts more than a few weeks. Seek urgent care after a fall onto the hand if the wrist looks bent or you cannot grip."),
    ("wrist_pain_lifting", "Wrist Pain When Lifting", "NHS - Wrist pain", "https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/hand-pain/wrist-pain/", "Wrist Pain", "self_care",
     "Pain at the base of the thumb or across the wrist when lifting a kettle or a child is often tendon irritation or strain. A click with pain after a twist can mean a ligament has been stressed.",
     "Use two hands to lift, keep the wrist in a neutral position, and rest from repeated gripping. A simple wrist splint for short periods can help some people.",
     "See a doctor if lifting pain does not settle in two weeks or the thumb base swells. Seek urgent care for sudden deformity after a fall."),
    ("ankle_pain_walking", "Ankle Pain When Walking", "NHS - Sprains and strains", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/sprains-and-strains/", "Ankle Pain", "self_care",
     "Ankle pain when walking often follows a twist, uneven ground, or worn shoes. A mild sprain, irritated tendons, or early arthritis after old injuries are common explanations. Swelling around the bone knobs is typical after a sprain.",
     "Rest from running, ice, a supportive shoe or brace, and elevation reduce swelling. Start gentle circles when the first sharp pain eases. Do not hop on a very swollen ankle.",
     "See a doctor if you cannot take four steps, there is bruising and a large swelling, or pain lasts more than two weeks. Seek urgent care if the ankle looks out of place."),
    ("heel_pain_first_steps", "Heel Pain on First Steps", "NHS - Heel pain", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/heel-pain/", "Heel Pain", "self_care",
     "Sharp heel pain with the first steps in the morning, or after sitting, is a classic description of plantar fasciitis. People say my heel hurts when I take my first steps. The band of tissue under the foot is irritated where it meets the heel bone.",
     "Calf stretches, supportive shoes instead of walking barefoot on hard floors, and rolling a cold bottle under the arch often help over weeks. Avoid long runs until the morning stab eases.",
     "See a doctor if heel pain lasts more than a few weeks or you have numbness in the foot. Seek urgent care if you cannot push off after a sudden snap in the Achilles area."),
    ("foot_pain_after_standing", "Foot Pain After Standing", "NHS - Foot pain", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/foot-pain/", "Foot Pain", "self_care",
     "Aching feet after standing at work can come from tired small muscles, flat or very high arches, or unsupportive shoes. The ball of the foot or the arch usually aches and eases when you sit.",
     "Change shoes for ones with cushioning, sit for short breaks, and stretch the calves. Compression socks help some people who also have ankle swelling by evening.",
     "See a doctor for foot pain with numbness, an open sore if you have diabetes, or pain that does not ease overnight. Seek urgent care for a suddenly cold, pale, or pulseless foot."),
    ("pain_after_exercise", "Muscle Soreness After Exercise", "NHS - Sprains and strains", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/sprains-and-strains/", "Muscle Soreness", "self_care",
     "A dull muscle ache that starts a day after a new or harder workout is delayed onset muscle soreness. It is common in the thighs, calves, or arms and eases within a few days. It is not the same as a sudden snap during sport.",
     "Gentle walking, light stretching, sleep, and fluids help. You can train other body parts while the sore muscles recover. Sudden sharp pain during a lift is a strain, not ordinary soreness.",
     "See a doctor if soreness lasts more than a week, the muscle swells a lot, or your urine turns dark after extreme exercise. Seek urgent care for a muscle that bunches up after a pop."),
    ("muscle_pain_after_sitting", "Aching Muscles After Sitting a Long Time", "NHS - Back pain", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/back-pain/", "Muscle Ache", "self_care",
     "Hips, back, and shoulders often ache after a long sit because muscles stay shortened and then suddenly stretch when you stand. This is a common everyday complaint, not automatically arthritis.",
     "Stand every 30 to 45 minutes, walk a short loop, and change position before pain starts. A firmer chair and both feet on the floor reduce the ache.",
     "See a doctor if sitting pain is paired with unexplained weight loss, fever, or night sweats. Seek emergency care for back pain with weak legs or loss of bladder control."),
    ("joint_stiffness_morning", "Morning Joint Stiffness", "NIAMS - Arthritis", "https://www.niams.nih.gov/health-topics/arthritis", "Joint Stiffness", "consult_doctor",
     "Stiff joints that loosen after you move around can be early osteoarthritis. Stiffness that lasts more than 30 to 60 minutes and affects many joints, especially the hands, is more typical of inflammatory arthritis and needs a medical review.",
     "Warmth, gentle movement before getting out of bed, and staying active through the day help mechanical stiffness. Do not ignore swelling of several joints.",
     "See a doctor if morning stiffness lasts more than half an hour most days, or joints look swollen. Seek urgent care for a single hot joint with fever."),
    ("night_leg_cramps", "Leg Cramps at Night", "NHS - Leg cramps", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/leg-cramps/", "Night Leg Cramps", "self_care",
     "People ask why do my legs cramp at night. A sudden tight knot in the calf or foot that wakes you is a common night cramp. Tired muscles, dehydration, pregnancy, and some medicines can play a part. Most night cramps are not dangerous.",
     "Straighten the knee and pull the toes toward you, then walk around. Regular calf stretches before bed, enough fluid in the day, and loose bedding that does not point the toes can reduce attacks.",
     "See a doctor if cramps happen most nights, you have muscle weakness, or swelling in one leg. Seek urgent care for a swollen, hot, painful calf, which may be a clot rather than a cramp."),
    ("headache_with_nausea", "Headache With Nausea", "NHS - Migraine", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/migraine/", "Headache With Nausea", "consult_doctor",
     "A throbbing headache with nausea, and sometimes sensitivity to light or sound, is a common migraine pattern. People ask what can cause a headache with nausea. Tension headaches can also make you feel sick if they are severe, but migraine is more often one-sided and pulsing.",
     "Rest in a quiet dark room, sip water, and use the pain relief your clinician has advised if you already have a plan. Keep a note of foods, sleep, and screens that seem to trigger attacks.",
     "See a doctor for new headaches with nausea that keep returning. Seek emergency care for a sudden thunderclap headache, a weak face or arm, confusion, or a headache with fever and a stiff neck."),
    ("headache_after_screens", "Headache After Looking at Screens", "NHS - Headaches", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/headaches/", "Headache", "self_care",
     "A tight band around the head after long screen time often goes with dry eyes, a forward neck, and skipped water or meals. This is usually a tension-type or eye-strain headache, not a sign of a serious brain problem on its own.",
     "Use the 20-20-20 eye rest, blink more, raise the screen, and drink water. A short walk outdoors can reset both the neck and the eyes.",
     "See a doctor if screen headaches become daily or you need glasses checked. Seek emergency care for a sudden severe headache unlike your usual ones."),
    ("dizziness_on_standing", "Light-Headedness When Standing Up", "NHS - Low blood pressure (hypotension)", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/low-blood-pressure-hypotension/", "Light-Headedness", "self_care",
     "Feeling woozy or seeing spots when you stand quickly is often a brief drop in blood pressure. Dehydration, a hot room, some blood pressure tablets, and standing after a long sit make it more likely.",
     "Stand up slowly, sit on the edge of the bed first, and drink extra fluid unless a doctor has limited your fluids. Compression stockings help some people.",
     "See a doctor if fainting happens more than once or you are on new medicine. Seek emergency care if light-headedness comes with chest pain, breathlessness, or an irregular pounding heartbeat."),
    ("tingling_hands", "Tingling in the Hands", "NHS - Pins and needles", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/pins-and-needles/", "Hand Tingling", "consult_doctor",
     "Pins and needles in the fingers can come from a bent wrist in sleep, leaning on an elbow, or pressure on a nerve in the neck. Carpal tunnel often affects the thumb, index, and middle fingers and may wake you at night.",
     "Change hand position, shake the hands out, and avoid prolonged pressure on the wrist. A night splint can help if a clinician thinks carpal tunnel is likely.",
     "See a doctor if tingling lasts, is only on one side, or comes with weakness. Seek emergency care for sudden face, arm, or speech changes, which can be a stroke warning."),
    ("numbness_foot", "Numbness in a Foot", "NHS - Peripheral neuropathy", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/peripheral-neuropathy/", "Foot Numbness", "consult_doctor",
     "A numb or burning foot can follow sitting on a nerve, tight shoes, or, when lasting, nerve irritation from the back or blood sugar problems. Both feet becoming numb over time needs a medical check.",
     "Do not ignore foot numbness if you have diabetes. Check shoes for stones, and inspect the skin daily for unnoticed sores.",
     "See a doctor for numbness that does not settle or affects walking. Seek urgent care for a suddenly weak leg, or a numb foot that is also cold and pale."),
    ("difficulty_sleeping", "Trouble Falling Asleep", "NHS - Insomnia", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/insomnia/", "Insomnia", "self_care",
     "Lying awake for a long time, or waking too early, is common during stress, late caffeine, irregular bedtimes, or pain. A few poor nights do not mean a lifelong sleep disease.",
     "Keep a regular get-up time, dim screens before bed, and use the bed for sleep rather than scrolling. Short daytime activity and less caffeine after mid-afternoon help many people.",
     "See a doctor if poor sleep lasts more than a few weeks, you snore and stop breathing, or low mood is also present. Do not start sleeping tablets without advice."),
    ("daytime_fatigue", "Tiredness Most Days", "NHS - Tiredness and fatigue", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/tiredness-and-fatigue/", "Fatigue", "consult_doctor",
     "Feeling washed out most days can follow poor sleep, low iron, thyroid problems, low mood, infection recovery, or simply too little rest. Tiredness alone does not name one disease.",
     "A regular sleep window, meals, daylight, and gentle activity are safe first steps. Note any weight change, thirst, or shortness of breath to tell a clinician.",
     "See a doctor if tiredness lasts more than a few weeks or you have pale skin, heavy periods, chest pain, or unexplained weight loss."),
    ("dry_cough_at_night", "Dry Cough at Night", "NHS - Cough", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/cough/", "Dry Cough", "self_care",
     "A tickly dry cough that gets worse at night is common after a cold, with post-nasal drip, reflux, or dry air. People say I have a dry cough at night. A dry cough after a viral illness can linger for weeks.",
     "Sit more upright to sleep, sip warm drinks, use honey if you are over one year old, and avoid smoke. A humidifier can help in a dry room. Antibiotics do not treat a simple viral cough.",
     "See a doctor if a cough lasts more than three to four weeks, you cough blood, or you have breathlessness, chest pain, or night sweats. Seek emergency care if you cannot catch your breath."),
    ("cough_with_mucus", "Cough With Mucus", "NHS - Cough", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/cough/", "Cough With Mucus", "self_care",
     "A chesty cough that brings up clear or yellow mucus often follows a cold or bronchitis. Green mucus alone does not prove you need antibiotics. Fever and feeling very unwell matter more.",
     "Fluids, rest, and sitting upright help mucus move. Steam from a hot shower can loosen a tight chest. Do not give cough medicine to young children without advice.",
     "See a doctor if mucus is bloody, the cough lasts more than three weeks, or you have a high fever and feel very ill. Seek emergency care for blue lips or severe breathlessness."),
    ("sore_throat_and_fever", "Sore Throat and Fever", "NHS - Sore throat", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/sore-throat/", "Sore Throat", "self_care",
     "A sore throat with fever is common with viral infections. People say I have a fever and sore throat. Swallowing hurts, the neck may feel tender, and you may also have a runny nose or cough. Strep throat is more likely without a cough and with tender neck glands, but only a clinician can decide if a test is needed.",
     "Rest, cool drinks, and simple pain relief if safe for you usually help. Most sore throats improve within a week. Antibiotics are not needed for most viral throats.",
     "See a doctor if you cannot swallow saliva, the fever lasts more than three days, or a rash appears. Seek emergency care for drooling, noisy breathing, or a very swollen neck."),
    ("runny_nose_sneezing", "Runny Nose and Sneezing", "CDC - Common Colds", "https://www.cdc.gov/antibiotic-use/colds.html", "Runny Nose", "self_care",
     "A runny nose with sneezing can be a cold or allergies. Itchiness in the nose and eyes without fever points more toward allergy. A cold more often brings a sore throat and lasts about a week.",
     "Saline sprays, tissues, and rest help a cold. For allergies, reducing dust or pollen exposure and pharmacy antihistamines may help if they are suitable for you.",
     "See a doctor if a runny nose lasts more than ten days with facial pain, or you have wheezing. Seek emergency care for swelling of the lips or tongue, or trouble breathing."),
    ("nasal_congestion", "Blocked Nose", "NHS - Catarrh", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/catarrh/", "Nasal Congestion", "self_care",
     "A stuffy nose makes it hard to breathe through one or both sides. Colds, allergies, and swollen turbinates from long use of decongestant sprays are common causes. Sleep is often worse.",
     "Saline rinses, steam, and sleeping slightly upright can ease a blocked nose. Do not use decongestant sprays for more than a few days, as they can worsen rebound blockage.",
     "See a doctor for blockage on one side only that lasts, or a blocked nose with a bad smell or bleeding. Seek urgent care after a head injury if fluid leaks from the nose."),
    ("sinus_pressure_face", "Sinus Pressure in the Face", "NHS - Sinusitis", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/sinusitis-sinus-infection/", "Sinus Discomfort", "self_care",
     "Pressure around the cheeks, eyes, or forehead that is worse when you bend forward often follows a cold. The sinuses can fill with mucus. Most cases are viral and settle without antibiotics.",
     "Fluids, steam, saline rinses, and simple pain relief help. Sleep with an extra pillow. Most sinus discomfort eases within two to three weeks.",
     "See a doctor if facial pain lasts more than three weeks, you have a high fever, or swelling around one eye. Seek emergency care for a swollen red eyelid with vision change or a severe headache."),
    ("earache_after_cold", "Earache After a Cold", "NHS - Earache", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/earache/", "Earache", "self_care",
     "An earache after a blocked nose is often pressure from the tube that joins the ear to the throat, or a middle-ear infection. Pain can be sharp and worse when lying down. Children get this more often than adults.",
     "Simple pain relief, sitting more upright, and treating the blocked nose with saline can help. Most earaches improve in a few days.",
     "See a doctor if ear pain lasts more than three days, fluid drains, or hearing drops suddenly. Seek urgent care for a swollen, very tender bone behind the ear, or a high fever with a stiff neck."),
    ("ear_congestion", "Blocked or Full Ear", "NHS - Catarrh", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/catarrh/", "Ear Congestion", "self_care",
     "A full or muffled ear after a flight, a cold, or water in the canal is common. Wax, eustachian tube blockage, or water trapped after swimming can all cause the same stuffed feeling.",
     "Yawning, swallowing, and saline for the nose can open the tube. Do not poke cotton buds into the ear. Pharmacy wax drops may help if wax is likely.",
     "See a doctor if the ear stays blocked for more than a week or you have hearing loss, dizziness, or discharge. Seek urgent care after a slap or explosion injury to the ear."),
    ("hoarse_voice", "Hoarse Voice", "NHS - Laryngitis", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/laryngitis/", "Hoarseness", "self_care",
     "A croaky or whispered voice after shouting, a cold, or reflux irritation is usually laryngitis. The voice box is inflamed. Most cases settle with voice rest.",
     "Rest the voice, drink fluids, and avoid whispering, which can strain the cords more. Steam can soothe. Do not smoke.",
     "See a doctor if hoarseness lasts more than three weeks, especially if you smoke. Seek urgent care for noisy breathing or difficulty swallowing saliva."),
    ("heartburn_after_meals", "Heartburn After Meals", "NHS - Heartburn and acid reflux", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/heartburn-and-acid-reflux/", "Heartburn", "self_care",
     "A burning rise behind the breastbone after a large, spicy, or late meal is typical heartburn from acid reflux. People may also taste sour fluid. It is common and not automatically a heart attack, but chest pain still needs care if it is new or severe.",
     "Eat smaller meals, avoid lying down for three hours after food, raise the head of the bed, and limit coffee, alcohol, and very fatty food. Pharmacy antacids may help if they are suitable for you.",
     "See a doctor if heartburn happens most weeks or you have trouble swallowing. Seek emergency care for crushing chest pain, pain into the arm or jaw, or breathlessness."),
    ("indigestion_bloating", "Bloating After Eating", "NHS - Bloating", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/bloating/", "Bloating", "self_care",
     "A tight, swollen tummy after eating is often gas, a large meal, or sensitivity to certain foods such as beans, onions, or fizzy drinks. People say my stomach feels bloated after eating. Occasional bloating is common and not a disease by itself.",
     "Eat more slowly, reduce fizzy drinks, and notice if dairy or very fatty meals make it worse. A short walk after eating can move gas. Sudden persistent bloating, especially in women over 50, needs a medical check.",
     "See a doctor if bloating is daily, you lose weight, or you have blood in the stool. Seek urgent care for sudden severe tummy pain with vomiting and no stool or wind."),
    ("stomach_cramps_after_food", "Stomach Cramps After Food", "NHS - Indigestion", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/indigestion/", "Stomach Cramps", "self_care",
     "Cramping in the upper tummy after meals can be indigestion, a rich meal, or a sensitive gut. Pain that eases after passing wind or stool is often gut-related. Pain that wakes you at night needs a review.",
     "Smaller meals, less alcohol, and sitting up after eating help many people. Keep a simple food and symptom note for a week.",
     "See a doctor for cramps lasting more than a few weeks, black stools, or vomiting blood. Seek emergency care for sudden severe abdominal pain."),
    ("nausea_without_vomiting", "Nausea Without Vomiting", "NHS - Feeling sick (nausea)", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/feeling-sick-nausea/", "Nausea", "self_care",
     "Feeling sick without being sick can follow a migraine, reflux, pregnancy, a new medicine, anxiety, or a mild stomach bug. Nausea alone does not name one cause.",
     "Sip water, eat small plain snacks if you can, and rest. Fresh air and avoiding strong smells help some people. Do not take leftover antibiotics for nausea.",
     "See a doctor if nausea lasts more than a few days, you cannot keep fluids down, or you are pregnant and cannot eat. Seek emergency care for a sudden severe headache with vomiting, or chest pain."),
    ("loss_of_appetite", "Loss of Appetite", "MedlinePlus - Appetite - decreased", "https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/003121.htm", "Loss of Appetite", "consult_doctor",
     "Not wanting food for a day or two during a cold or stress is common. Lasting loss of appetite with weight loss needs a medical look, because infection, low mood, medicines, and other illnesses can all reduce hunger.",
     "Offer small regular meals, fluids, and foods you still enjoy. Do not force large plates. Note any fever, pain, or swallowing trouble.",
     "See a doctor if appetite stays low for more than two weeks or clothes become loose. Seek urgent care for vomiting blood or black stools."),
    ("fever_and_body_aches", "Fever and Body Aches", "CDC - Flu Symptoms", "https://www.cdc.gov/flu/symptoms/symptoms.htm", "Fever and Body Aches", "self_care",
     "Fever with aching muscles is common in flu and other viral illnesses. You may also have a headache, dry cough, and feel washed out. Most otherwise healthy adults improve with rest.",
     "Rest, fluids, and simple fever reducers if they are safe for you can ease symptoms. Stay home while feverish to reduce spread. Antibiotics do not treat flu viruses.",
     "See a doctor if fever lasts more than three days or you have chest pain or confusion. Seek emergency care for difficulty breathing, blue lips, or a non-blanching rash with fever."),
    ("mild_dehydration_hot_day", "Thirst and Dark Urine on a Hot Day", "NHS - Dehydration", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/dehydration/", "Mild Dehydration", "self_care",
     "Thirst, a dry mouth, headache, and darker urine after heat, exercise, or a busy day without drinks are early dehydration signs. Older adults may feel less thirst, so they can dry out faster.",
     "Drink water or an oral rehydration drink in small sips. Rest in a cool place. Water-rich foods such as fruit also help.",
     "Seek urgent care if you have no urine for eight hours, feel confused, or cannot keep fluids down, and for any unwell baby or frail older person."),
    ("chills_without_high_fever", "Chills and Feeling Cold", "MedlinePlus - Chills", "https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/003091.htm", "Chills", "self_care",
     "Shivering or goosebumps can start as a fever is rising, after getting wet, or from anxiety. Chills with a new infection often come before you notice a high temperature.",
     "Warm layers, fluids, and rest help. Check your temperature if you feel unwell. Isolated chills after a cold shower are not an illness.",
     "See a doctor if chills keep returning with night sweats or weight loss. Seek emergency care for chills with a very stiff neck, confusion, or a spreading purple rash."),
    ("dry_itchy_skin", "Dry Itchy Skin", "NHS - Itchy skin", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/itchy-skin/", "Dry Itchy Skin", "self_care",
     "Dry, tight, itchy skin is common in cold weather, after hot showers, or with soap that strips oil. Eczema and ageing skin also itch. Widespread itching without a rash can have other causes and needs a check if it lasts.",
     "Use lukewarm water, pat dry, and put on a plain moisturiser while the skin is still damp. Avoid scratching until the skin bleeds. Fragrance-free products are kinder.",
     "See a doctor if itching keeps you awake, a rash spreads, or you have yellow skin or unexplained weight loss. Seek urgent care for a sudden widespread rash with lip swelling."),
    ("mild_rash_after_heat", "Heat Rash", "NHS - Heat rash (prickly heat)", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/heat-rash-prickly-heat/", "Heat Rash", "self_care",
     "Tiny itchy bumps in skin folds after sweating are often heat rash. The sweat ducts are blocked. It is common on the neck, chest, and groin in hot weather.",
     "Cool the skin, wear loose cotton, and avoid thick creams that trap heat. The rash usually fades in a few days.",
     "See a doctor if the bumps become painful, filled with pus, or you have a fever. Seek urgent care for a rapidly spreading painful red area."),
    ("insect_bite_itchy", "Itchy Insect Bite", "NHS - Insect bites and stings", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/insect-bites-and-stings/", "Insect Bite", "self_care",
     "A small raised itchy bump after a mosquito or other bite is a local reaction. Redness of a coin size is common. A sting can be more painful at first.",
     "Wash with soap and water, use a cold pack, and avoid scratching. Pharmacy antihistamine cream or tablets may help if suitable for you.",
     "Seek emergency care for lip or tongue swelling, wheeze, or faintness after a sting. See a doctor if redness spreads over days with fever, which can be infection."),
    ("minor_sprain_self_care", "Minor Sprain Self-Care", "NHS - Sprains and strains", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/sprains-and-strains/", "Minor Sprain", "self_care",
     "A minor sprain is a stretched ligament after a twist. The area is sore, mildly swollen, and you can still take some weight. Ankles and wrists are common sites.",
     "Protect the joint, rest from sport, ice for short spells, a light bandage, and elevation for the first two days. Then start gentle movement so the joint does not stiffen.",
     "See a doctor if you cannot take four steps, there is a large bruise and swelling, or pain is not improving after a week. Seek urgent care if the joint looks crooked."),
    ("burning_when_urinating", "Burning When You Urinate", "NHS - Urinary tract infection", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/urinary-tract-infections-utis/", "Painful Urination", "consult_doctor",
     "A burning sting when you pass urine, with a need to go often and only a little urine, is a common urinary tract infection pattern. People say it burns when I urinate. Other causes include irritation after sex, some soaps, and, in men, prostate or urethra inflammation.",
     "Drink extra water, do not hold urine, and see a clinician rather than guessing antibiotics. Pain relief and a hot water bottle on the lower tummy can ease discomfort while you wait.",
     "See a doctor or clinic the same day for burning urine, especially if you are pregnant. Seek urgent care for fever, shaking, back pain, or vomiting, which can mean a kidney infection."),
    ("frequent_urination", "Passing Urine Very Often", "NHS - Urinary tract infections (UTIs)", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/urinary-tract-infections-utis/", "Frequent Urination", "consult_doctor",
     "Needing to urinate many times in the day or night can follow a urine infection, drinking more fluid, caffeine, pregnancy, a large prostate, or higher blood sugar. Frequency alone does not name one disease.",
     "Note how much you drink, whether nights are interrupted, and if there is burning or blood. Reduce evening caffeine as a first step if there is no pain.",
     "See a doctor if frequency lasts more than a few days, you are thirsty all the time, or you have fever. Seek urgent care if you suddenly cannot pass urine at all."),
    ("period_pain_everyday", "Period Cramps", "NHS - Period pain", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/period-pain/", "Menstrual Cramps", "self_care",
     "Cramping in the lower tummy and back around the start of a period is common. The womb lining is shedding. Pain can also radiate to the thighs. Most period pain is not dangerous, but pain that is new and very severe needs a review.",
     "Heat on the tummy, gentle movement, and simple pain relief if safe for you help many people. A warm bath can ease the first day.",
     "See a doctor if period pain stops you working, is getting worse over months, or you have very heavy bleeding. Seek urgent care for sudden one-sided pelvic pain with fainting."),
    ("vaginal_itch_discharge", "Vaginal Itch or Discharge Change", "NHS - Vaginal discharge", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vaginal-discharge/", "Vaginal Symptoms", "consult_doctor",
     "Itch with a thick white discharge can be thrush. A thin grey discharge with a fishy smell can be bacterial vaginosis. Green or yellow discharge with pelvic pain needs a sexual-health check. These are possibilities, not a self-diagnosis.",
     "Avoid scented soaps and tight synthetic underwear. Do not douche. A pharmacist or clinic can advise on suitable treatment after questions about your symptoms.",
     "See a clinic or doctor for a first episode, pregnancy, pelvic pain, fever, or symptoms after a new partner. Seek urgent care for severe pelvic pain and fainting."),
    ("toothache_sudden", "Sudden Toothache", "NHS - Toothache", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/toothache/", "Toothache", "consult_doctor",
     "A sharp or throbbing tooth pain when biting or with cold drinks often means decay, a cracked filling, or an irritated nerve. Swelling of the gum next to one tooth can be an abscess starting.",
     "Rinse with warm salt water, use simple pain relief if safe, and avoid very hot or cold drinks. See a dentist; antibiotics alone do not fix decay.",
     "See a dentist as soon as you can. Seek urgent care for a swollen face or eye, difficulty swallowing, or a high fever with a dental swelling."),
    ("gum_pain_bleeding", "Sore or Bleeding Gums", "NHS - Gum disease", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/gum-disease/", "Gum Pain", "consult_doctor",
     "Gums that bleed when you brush and feel swollen are often gingivitis from plaque. They can look red and tender. Bleeding is a sign to clean more carefully, not to stop brushing.",
     "Brush twice a day along the gum line, clean between teeth, and see a dentist or hygienist. Smoking makes gum disease worse.",
     "See a dentist for lasting gum pain or loose teeth. Seek urgent care for rapidly spreading facial swelling."),
    ("bad_breath_lasting", "Lasting Bad Breath", "NHS - Bad breath (halitosis)", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/bad-breath/", "Bad Breath", "self_care",
     "Ongoing bad breath is often from the tongue, gum disease, dry mouth, or food leftover. Occasional morning breath is normal. Lasting odour that others notice needs a dental check.",
     "Brush the tongue, clean between teeth, drink water, and avoid smoking. Sugar-free gum can help saliva if your dentist agrees.",
     "See a dentist if bad breath lasts despite cleaning. See a doctor if you also have a lasting dry mouth, sinus infection, or unexpected weight loss."),
    ("red_eye_irritation", "Red Irritated Eye", "NHS - Red eye", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/red-eye/", "Red Eye", "self_care",
     "A pink or red eye with grit, watering, or mild itch is often conjunctivitis or dryness. Vision is usually normal. Pain, light hurting a lot, or a cloudy spot on the eye is more serious.",
     "Wash hands, do not share towels, and use lubricating drops if they are suitable. Remove contact lenses until the eye is better.",
     "See a clinician the same day for a painful red eye, light sensitivity, or reduced vision. Seek urgent care after a chemical splash or a welding flash."),
    ("dry_eyes_screens", "Dry Eyes From Screens", "NHS - Dry eyes", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/dry-eyes/", "Dry Eyes", "self_care",
     "Gritty, burning, or watery eyes after screen work happen because you blink less. The tear film dries. Air conditioning makes it worse.",
     "Look away every 20 minutes, blink fully, and use preservative-free lubricating drops if a pharmacist agrees. Raise screens so you look slightly down.",
     "See an optometrist or doctor if drops do not help, or you have light pain and reduced vision. Contact-lens wearers should stop lenses until reviewed."),
    ("stress_tight_shoulders", "Tight Shoulders From Stress", "NHS - Stress", "https://www.nhs.uk/mental-health/feelings-symptoms-behaviours/feelings-and-symptoms/stress/", "Stress-Related Muscle Tension", "self_care",
     "Worry can show up as tight shoulders, a clenched jaw, a tension headache, or an upset stomach. The body stays in a ready state. These physical feelings are real even when tests are normal.",
     "Short walks, slower breathing, regular sleep, and talking to someone you trust can lower the tightness. Heat on the shoulders helps some people.",
     "See a doctor or mental-health service if stress stops you working or sleeping for weeks. Seek urgent help if you have chest pain with breathlessness, or thoughts of harming yourself."),
    ("mild_anxiety_physical", "Physical Feelings of Mild Anxiety", "NHS - Anxiety", "https://www.nhs.uk/mental-health/conditions/generalised-anxiety-disorder-gad/", "Mild Anxiety Symptoms", "self_care",
     "Anxiety can cause a racing heart, a tight chest, shaky hands, a dry mouth, and a need to use the toilet. These feelings can mimic some medical problems, so new or severe chest pain still needs a medical check.",
     "Slow breathing, naming five things you can see, and reducing extra caffeine can settle a mild wave. Regular sleep and daylight help the next day.",
     "See a doctor to rule out other causes if symptoms are new, and for talking therapies if worry is daily. Seek emergency care for crushing chest pain or fainting."),
    ("iron_deficiency_symptoms", "Possible Iron Deficiency Symptoms", "NHS - Iron deficiency anaemia", "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/iron-deficiency-anaemia/", "Iron Deficiency Symptoms", "consult_doctor",
     "Tiredness, looking paler than usual, breathlessness on stairs, and restless legs can happen when iron stores are low. Heavy periods and a low-iron diet are common reasons. Symptoms overlap with many other conditions, so a blood test is needed.",
     "Do not start high-dose iron tablets on a guess, because too much iron can be harmful. A clinician can advise on diet and whether tablets are needed.",
     "See a doctor for lasting tiredness, heavy periods, or black stools. Seek urgent care for chest pain or fainting with severe paleness."),
]

